**Read:** Finish the assigned Roylance modules and problems, Bower's relevant constitutive/failure chapters, Cambridge packages and MIT 3.22 published exercises/exams. Complete the family readings, including MIT polymer lectures and Wood Handbook chapters 3–7 and 10–13.

The following three studies all contribute to this section: polymer history and inference; brittle/composite fracture and architecture; and metallic/cellular mechanisms across scales. They can be pursued in different orders and meet again at the common review. Use complete primary studies with accessible methods and data whenever available. Record exactly what was read, reproduced, measured and still inferred.

**Work:** Choose a useful material question for each study. Make competing predictions before inspecting all the observations, then revise them. Include a derivation, an independent calculation, a source-data analysis and a justified proposed or performed experiment. A proposed experiment remains a proposal until it is actually performed; do not invent data to make a narrative complete.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
**Continue in materials engineering:** [Advanced study of polymer history and inference](Advanced-study-of-polymer-history-and-inference.lingua#r_4A2JX239ZXRJ46XB09TH4G0F6V), [Advanced study of brittle and composite response](Advanced-study-of-brittle-and-composite-response.lingua#r_5QKK03KCFB5D19PXR5D4M6W4R5), [Advanced study of metallic and cellular mechanisms](Advanced-study-of-metallic-and-cellular-mechanisms.lingua#r_6G1GVJ62N1YXJM0ZMYZ10XM1XN).

